# C / C++ security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to C/C++
memory-safety and undefined-behavior risks, and the sanitizers/safe APIs
to use instead. Applies to both C and C++ files.

## Memory safety

- Never dereference, write through, or `free` a pointer after it has been
  freed or has gone out of scope (use-after-free) — set a freed pointer
  to `NULL` when it could be read again, and never return a pointer or
  reference to a stack-local object.
- Never `free`/`delete` the same pointer twice (double-free) — a
  freed-and-nulled pointer makes a second `free`/`delete` a harmless
  no-op instead of heap corruption.
- Bounds-check every array/buffer index and every pointer arithmetic
  expression built from untrusted input before it is used to read or
  write memory — an off-by-one or an unchecked length from user input is
  the most common route to a buffer overflow.
- Never use `strcpy`, `strcat`, `sprintf`, or `gets` on data whose length
  is not already statically known to fit the destination — use
  `snprintf`, a length-checked copy, or a `std::string`/`std::vector`
  that manages its own capacity.

## Undefined behavior

- Signed integer overflow is undefined behavior in C/C++ (unlike
  unsigned wraparound, which is well-defined modular arithmetic) — check
  bounds before an addition/multiplication that could overflow a signed
  type, or use an unsigned/wider type, or a checked-arithmetic helper,
  for a bounds or size computation derived from untrusted input.
- Never read an uninitialized local variable or heap allocation — value-
  initialize (`Type x{};` in C++, an explicit `= 0`/`memset` in C) any
  variable whose first use might be a read, and never rely on whatever
  value happened to be left on the stack or heap.
- Never access an object through a pointer of an incompatible type (a
  strict-aliasing violation) — use `memcpy` for a type-punning copy, or
  (C++20+) `std::bit_cast`, instead of reinterpreting a pointer's type
  and dereferencing it.
- Do not read past the end of an array via pointer arithmetic even when
  the read "usually" lands in valid memory — an out-of-bounds read past
  the last element is undefined behavior regardless of what happens to be
  mapped there.

## Concurrency

- Every variable read from more than one thread while at least one thread
  writes it is guarded by a mutex, or is a `std::atomic`/C11 `_Atomic`
  type — an unsynchronized shared read/write is a data race, which is
  undefined behavior in both C and C++, not merely "occasionally wrong."
- Acquire locks in a consistent global order across the codebase to avoid
  deadlock; prefer `std::lock_guard`/`std::scoped_lock` (which also
  supports locking multiple mutexes deadlock-free in one call) over
  manual `lock()`/`unlock()` pairs, since a manual pair skips the unlock
  on an early return or exception.

## Sanitizers

- Build and run with AddressSanitizer + UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer
  (`-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g`, Clang/GCC)
  whenever investigating a memory-safety or UB bug report, or before
  trusting a fix for one — ASan catches use-after-free, double-free,
  buffer overflow, and heap corruption at the exact faulting access; UBSan
  catches signed overflow, misaligned access, and other UB the compiler
  is otherwise free to miscompile around silently.
- Build and run with ThreadSanitizer (`-fsanitize=thread`) — built as a
  separate binary from ASan/UBSan, the two runtimes are not composable in
  one build — whenever investigating a suspected data race or before
  trusting a fix for one.
- A memory-safety, UB, or concurrency bug report is not actually verified
  fixed until it has been reproduced and re-run under the relevant
  sanitizer and comes back clean; a fix that only "looks right" by
  inspection, or that only passes without a sanitizer attached, has not
  been verified.

## Dependency and input hygiene

- Validate the length/format of any externally-sourced buffer (network,
  file, IPC, command-line argument) before parsing it — do not assume a
  length field in the input itself is trustworthy without a sanity check
  against the buffer's actual size.
- Never hard-code a credential, API key, or signing secret in source;
  load it from environment/secret storage the project already uses.
